<notes>
- V7: I read "respected" as `max_units <= capacity`, so equality passes. The measured value is the list of warehouse ids that exceeded capacity. If a warehouse appears in several entries, it is listed once per offending entry.
- V8: I read "notified" as an exact match of the whole `[sku, wh, qty]` triple in the same entry's `notified` list. A quantity mismatch counts as not notified. Matching is per entry, so a notification in one entry does not cover an expiry in another. If the spec only requires matching on `(sku, wh)`, or matching across entries, this needs changing. The measured value is the list of unnotified expired items.
- V9: The spec did not give the range. I assumed inclusive bounds of 0 to 100, held in `RELIABILITY_MIN` and `RELIABILITY_MAX` so they are easy to change. Please confirm the real range. The measured value is the list of supplier ids outside the range.
- Tests use `unittest` from the standard library, following CONTRIBUTING. They assume `validators_later.py` is importable from the repository root and that a `tests/` directory is acceptable. I have not run them.
- I did not touch `validators.py` or `vendor/`.
</notes>
